- op: discuss  # 6f5c
  at: '2026-07-31T03:26:37Z'  # 6f5c
  by: agent  # 6f5c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6f5c
  clock: 3  # 6f5c
  nonce: 6f5c  # 6f5c
  message: "PREMISE CHECK (live measurement, dev 1cb6c50d04) — the filed framing is wrong on the SCOPE AXIS, and the root cause is one level deeper than filed. Substrate was a SELF-ANALYSIS snapshot (hypergumbo on hypergumbo at 9a18959071), which is what produced the misleading counts.\n\nMethod: generated a uniform multi-language probe corpus into /tmp (NOT in-tree, per the fixture-in-place trap), one file per language declaring an enum with variants, a trait/interface/protocol with members, and a module; ran survey; counted contains-out per (language, container-kind). Positive control present and green (class/file/struct/interface at 100%), so the nulls are real rather than a dead harness. First run of this probe reported 0 contains edges for EVERY kind including class -- that was my measurement being wrong (the artifact field is \"type\", not \"edge_type\"), corrected before drawing any conclusion.\n\nMEASURED, by (language, container-kind):\n- enum: rust 0%, scala 0%, typescript 0%, csharp 0%, cpp 0%, kotlin 0% -- but java 100% and swift 100%.\n- trait: rust 0% -- but scala 100%.\n- module: ruby 100%. Over 20 analyzers emit kind=module today.\n- NOT IN THE FILED SCOPE AT ALL, and equally broken: typescript interface 0%, swift protocol 0%. C# interface, by contrast, is 100%.\n- Re-ran with multi-line container bodies to rule out a degenerate one-line-span fixture artifact; every gap persisted.\n\nCORRECTIONS TO THIS ITEM:\n1. \"module is untestable because the analyzer produces zero module-kind nodes\" is FALSE as a producer claim. It is an artifact of a Python self-analysis substrate taken about two weeks after INV-hojus collapsed Python module-kind into file-kind. Ruby module containment works at 100%.\n2. \"trait 0% / enum 0%\" is not a property of the KIND. Java enum, Swift enum, Scala trait and Ruby module all work. The real axis is (language, container-kind), so a per-kind scoping cannot express the defect and cannot close it.\n3. The item scopes to 3 kinds and treats interface (42.9%) as working. Measured, typescript interface and swift protocol are 0% -- two live holes the filing never looked at. The 42.9% was corpus dilution, not a partial mechanism.\n4. Neither the slicer nor the containment linker is at fault. The containment linker has handled trait/enum/struct containers since 2026-02-08 (1ecae423af) and correctly emits contains for every container whose members exist. The actual defect: the ANALYZERS EMIT NO MEMBER SYMBOL AT ALL for those bodies. Verified by span-containment: for rust enum, rust trait, scala enum, ts enum, ts interface, swift protocol, csharp enum, cpp enum and kotlin enum-class, zero symbols exist anywhere inside the container span. There is no missing edge; there is a missing node.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (the filed repro, reproduced, with an in-run positive control):\n  hypergumbo slice --entry Color --language java --reverse   -> nodes: 2   (expansion fires)\n  hypergumbo slice --entry Color --language rust --reverse   -> nodes: 1   (container only)\n  hypergumbo slice --entry Color --language scala --reverse  -> nodes: 1   (container only)\nNote that rust/ts/swift Drawable return 2 nodes, but via implements edges, NOT via class expansion -- so a naive node-count check on a trait would read as a false pass.\n\nNOT A RECORDED DECISION: no ADR, spec passage or doc anywhere states that enum variants or interface/protocol member signatures are deliberately not symbols. And the in-tree precedent runs the other way -- d_lang.py::_process_enum_member and nim.py already emit enum variants as kind=field with dotted names specifically so the containment linker roots them (the WI-sakug / WI-jusus field-emission lineage).\n\nSPEC: docs/hypergumbo-spec.md:1214 carries the green delivered mark and names SEVEN containers (class, interface, module, struct, trait, enum, file) -- the filing says six, omitting file.\n\nREMEDY SHAPE (chokepoint + validator, not a per-site sweep): the enforcement belongs in the existing G2 emission-parity matrix gate (packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_emission_parity_matrix.py), whose uniform-injected-fixture methodology exists precisely so an empty cell means analyzer gap rather than construct-absence (WI-rubip). Its construct set has no container-with-members case, so this defect class is invisible to it; KNOWN_HOLES is currently empty, i.e. the matrix reports full parity while these holes are live. Adding the construct plus a member-emission column converts an ad-hoc probe into a standing lock. The per-analyzer emission fixes then drain against it."  # 6f5c
- op: discuss  # 532b
  at: '2026-07-31T03:38:08Z'  # 532b
  by: agent  # 532b
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 532b
  clock: 4  # 532b
  nonce: 532b  # 532b
  message: "CORRECTION to my own premise-check note above, plus PR #109 (merged, dev ff8e54181c).\n\nThe note claimed \"enum: ... but java 100% and swift 100%\". That is misleading and I am withdrawing it. It was measured on a probe corpus whose java and swift enum bodies each contained a METHOD (java `public String label()`, swift `func label()`). The containment linker rooted THAT method. It never rooted a single enum variant. My positive control was measuring an adjacent property.\n\nRe-measured at the analyzer level (run_analyzer in isolation, uniform fixtures, one enum per language declaring only named members):\n- emits_enum_members: typescript 0, java 0, rust 0, csharp 0, swift 0. ZERO of the five analyzers that emit an `enum` emit any of its variants.\n- emits_abstract_members: go OK, java OK, csharp OK; typescript 0, rust 0, swift 0.\n\nSo the enum gap is UNIVERSAL across the mainstream fleet, not the 5-of-7 split the earlier note implied. This makes the finding stronger, not weaker, but the earlier phrasing would have led a reader to believe java and swift were already correct and could be used as reference implementations. They cannot. The reference implementations are the NICHE analyzers: D and Nim (verified live: D `enum Color` -> field Color.red, field Color.green).\n\nThe general form, which is the second time this session that my own stated measurement was the artifact rather than the producer: a positive control has to exercise the EXACT property under test. A container that contains something is not evidence that a container contains its MEMBERS.\n\nSHIPPED (PR #109): the property is now locked per-(language, container-kind) as two columns on the existing G2 emission-parity matrix -- `emits_enum_members` and `emits_abstract_members` -- with the uniform construct added to all eight fixture languages. Two columns rather than one because the families fail independently; a merged column would let a language pass on its abstract members while its enum variants stayed invisible. The columns measure span nesting rather than `contains` edges, because the matrix runs one analyzer in isolation and `contains` is minted downstream -- the defect is a missing node, not a missing edge.\n\nNon-vacuity established in both directions: 3 hard passes (csharp/go/java emits_abstract_members) alongside 8 strict xfails. Holes are xfail(strict=True), never imperative pytest.xfail(), so fixing an analyzer XPASSes its cell and forces the entry's removal in the same PR.\n\ndocs/hypergumbo-spec.md keeps its delivered mark on the slicer mechanism (which genuinely expands all seven container kinds) and now names the currently-vacuous cells inline, so the mark no longer reads as an outcome guarantee.\n\nSCOPE NOT COVERED, stated explicitly: the matrix gates the eight core-availability fixture languages. The scala, cpp and kotlin enum gaps this investigation measured are real and are NOT gated -- they need grammar wheels the matrix deliberately does not require. Python and JavaScript are marked not-applicable with a written re-eval trigger (Python's enum and Protocol are both classes, already locked by emits_field; JavaScript has neither construct), as is Go's enum (a const block whose members are siblings of the type, not nested in its body).\n\nSTATUS: this item is NOT done. PR #109 locks the measurement and fixes no analyzer. Remaining work is the per-analyzer drain against the eight holes, starting with rust (the item's original headline -- both its enum and trait cells)."  # 532b
- op: discuss  # 8509
  at: '2026-07-31T04:34:34Z'  # 8509
  by: agent  # 8509
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8509
  clock: 5  # 8509
  nonce: '8509'  # 8509
  message: "RUST DRAINED (PR #110, merged, dev 9baaa9afbd). Item stays OPEN -- 6 of 8 holes remain.\n\nRust was the item's original headline (trait 0%, enum 0%) and had THREE distinct gaps behind that one symptom, not one:\n1. enum_variant produced no symbol at all -> now kind=\"field\" named Color::Red.\n2. function_signature_item (trait method, no default body) produced no symbol at all -> now kind=\"method\" named Drawable::draw.\n3. function_item INSIDE a trait_item (a method WITH a default body) DID produce a symbol, but as kind=\"function\" with a BARE name -- `area`, not `Drawable::area`. Indistinguishable from a module-level free function, and unrooted by the containment linker because its parent extraction needs a separator in the name.\n\nNumber 3 is the methodologically important one and is a caution for the remaining analyzers: it was ALREADY emitting a symbol inside the trait's span, so a check of the form \"does this container have any member\" passes against it. The G2 column measures span nesting, which is the right measurement for gap DETECTION but is not sufficient to certify a language as correct -- rust would have scored green on emits_abstract_members with a fixture whose trait methods carried default bodies. My fixtures use signature-only members, which is what makes the cell honest. Whoever drains typescript / swift / java / csharp should check the equivalent construct in each language (a TS interface method is always signature-only; a C# interface member can have a default implementation since C# 8; a Swift protocol can have an extension providing defaults) before trusting a green cell.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE (the filed repro, re-run, with an unchanged control):\n  slice --entry Color    --language rust --reverse   1 node  -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Drawable --language rust --reverse   2 nodes -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Color    --language java --reverse   2 nodes -> 2 nodes  (still a hole, correctly unmoved)\nNote the Drawable \"2 before\" came via implements edges, NOT class expansion, so a node-count check on a trait reads as a false pass. That is a second reason not to certify from counts alone.\n\nCROSS-MODULE BREAK CAUGHT BY AN EXISTING GATE: rust_scip.compute_rust_stable_id_from_source recomputes rust.py identities from source so the SCIP rust-analyzer pass and the tree-sitter pass can dedup (WI-zakub parity). It walked function_item only, so the moment rust.py emitted a trait-signature method the parity test failed with helper=None. This is a real defect rather than a test artifact -- rust-analyzer's SCIP emit does index trait method declarations, so these are exactly the symbols both passes see. Node-type filter and owner resolution are mirrored there now. Anyone draining another analyzer should check whether that language has a second identity producer with the same mirroring obligation.\n\nDESIGN NOTE: the trait-owner lookup is deliberately a SEPARATE helper from _get_impl_target rather than a widening of it. That helper has six callers, most in call/receiver resolution where it answers \"what type is self here\", and a trait is not a type a receiver can be -- widening it would have silently changed how those sites resolve. _get_trait_owner stops at the first impl_item because in `impl Drawable for Service` the methods belong to Service.\n\nREMAINING (6 holes, all still xfail(strict=True) so each drain forces its own promotion):\n  emits_enum_members:     typescript, java, csharp, swift   (4 of the 5 analyzers that emit an enum)\n  emits_abstract_members: typescript, swift                 (2 of 6 applicable)\n\nNOT GATED AND STILL BROKEN, stated so it is not mistaken for done: scala, cpp and kotlin enums. Measured in the premise check, outside the matrix because it covers only the eight core-availability fixture languages and those three need grammar wheels. Kotlin is the subtle one -- `enum class Color` is emitted as kind=\"class\", so it will not even appear under an enum-kind query."  # 8509
- op: discuss  # 1e17
  at: '2026-07-31T06:21:31Z'  # 1e17
  by: agent  # 1e17
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1e17
  clock: 6  # 1e17
  nonce: '1e17'  # 1e17
  message: "TYPESCRIPT (PR #111) AND SWIFT (PR #112) DRAINED. Item stays OPEN -- 2 holes remain (java, csharp enum members). emits_abstract_members is FULLY DRAINED.\n\nProgress on the eight holes this item opened:\n  emits_enum_members:     was ts/java/rust/csharp/swift (5)  ->  now java, csharp (2)\n  emits_abstract_members: was ts/rust/swift (3)              ->  now NONE\n\nTYPESCRIPT: the analyzer emitted CLASS members correctly (Sq.side, Sq.draw) but nothing inside enum or interface, so the gap was per-container rather than per-analyzer. Members now follow the analyzer's own class-member shape, Owner.member with \".\" Two interface members are deliberately NOT emitted: construct_signature (new (x: number): D) and index_signature ([key: string]: unknown) carry no property_identifier, so there is no name to anchor; skipping is a fails-safe recall miss, inventing a name would be a wrong-name phantom. Members are emitted from INSIDE the container's branch rather than as top-level node-type cases, so the owner is in hand and no span/ancestor walk is needed -- which is what stops an interface and an implementing class in the same file, with the same member names, from claiming each other's members.\n\nSWIFT, and the part that matters beyond this item:\n\nThe analyzer fix alone DID NOT MOVE THE OUTCOME. After swift.py began emitting protocol requirements, the isolated-analyzer tests were green and the emits_abstract_members column read green -- and the end-to-end reverse slice from a protocol was UNCHANGED at 2 nodes. In the full pipeline the requirements existed and were correctly named (Drawable.draw, Drawable.area) while the protocol scored contains_out=0, with a sibling enum and struct in the same file each rooting 3 members.\n\nCause: \"protocol\" was absent from linkers.containment.CONTAINER_KINDS and from the slicer's own _CONTAINER_KINDS. Swift/Obj-C protocol containment was therefore STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE regardless of what any analyzer emitted. It is a registered Symbol.kind emitted by both swift.py and objc.py. Added to both sets; slice --entry Drawable --language swift --reverse then goes 2 nodes -> 4.\n\nBEARING ON THIS ITEM'S OWN GATE, and on how its remaining holes should be closed: the G2 emission-parity columns measure ONE ANALYZER IN ISOLATION via span nesting. They cannot see the linker or the slicer, so a green cell is NOT evidence that reverse-slice expansion works for that language. Member emission is necessary but not sufficient -- the container's kind must ALSO be in containment's CONTAINER_KINDS and in the slicer's set. Whoever drains java or csharp should re-run the end-to-end repro rather than certify from the column. (java/csharp enums are \"class\"-adjacent kinds already present in both sets, so this specific trap is unlikely to repeat there -- but that is a prediction, not a measurement.)\n\nSWIFT ENUM DETAIL, a caution for the remaining analyzers: the analyzer already emitted an enum's METHODS and computed PROPERTIES, so an enum carrying one method looked healthy on any span-nesting probe while every one of its CASES was invisible. Also, \"case green, blue\" is a SINGLE enum_entry node carrying TWO simple_identifier children, so emission must iterate identifiers rather than entries; a per-entry loop drops every case after the first comma and leaves the container looking populated.\n\nSTALE COMMENTS CORRECTED (found by the analogue check, not by a gate): three comments named \"service\" as a container kind and none was true -- containment's module docstring, its CONTAINER_KINDS comment, and slice.py's comment, the last citing it as the justification for the slicer's set being narrower. The ADR-0027 Cluster-D fold made interface + meta[\"framework_role\"]=\"service\" canonical and proto.py emits exactly that, so no producer emits kind=\"service\".\n\nPROCESS NOTE, recorded because it silently corrupted a coverage measurement: the new swift test file was UNTRACKED, and smart-test builds its changed-file set from three git diff invocations, none of which report untracked files. The sole coverer of the new branch was absent from the manifest while three sibling swift test files were present, and the gate reported swift.py at 99% with 8 missing lines -- exactly the lines that file's own passing test exercises. Filed as a second instance on INV-kinin."  # 1e17
