- op: discuss  # 2283
  at: '2026-07-31T06:52:20Z'  # 2283
  by: agent  # 2283
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2283
  clock: 8  # 2283
  nonce: '2283'  # 2283
  message: "CLOSING as done. Five PRs: #109 (gate) then #110 rust, #111 typescript, #112 swift, #113 java/csharp. dev 5eb051e8c7.\n\nBEHAVIORAL EVIDENCE -- this item's own filed repro, re-run per language on the merged tree, not a proxy metric:\n  slice --entry Color    --language rust       --reverse   1 node  -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Drawable --language rust       --reverse   2 nodes -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Color    --language typescript --reverse   1 node  -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Drawable --language typescript --reverse   2 nodes -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Color    --language swift      --reverse   2 nodes -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Drawable --language swift      --reverse   2 nodes -> 4 nodes\n  slice --entry Color    --language java       --reverse   2 nodes -> 5 nodes\n  slice --entry Color    --language csharp     --reverse   1 node  -> 4 nodes\nRe-verified on dev 5eb051e8c7 by contains-out count on a freshly generated probe corpus: rust enum 3 / trait 2, typescript enum 3 / interface 3, csharp enum 3 / interface 2, swift protocol 2. The \"2 before\" figures for the abstract types came via implements edges rather than class expansion, so a node-count check on a trait or protocol would have read as a false pass -- which is why the counts above are paired with the contains-out measurement.\n\nWHAT THE ITEM ASKED FOR, both branches satisfied: its Expectation offered either \"each kind emits contains edges to its members\" OR \"the spec lists which kinds the expansion is vacuous on\". The first is now true for every gated (language, container-kind) pair, and the spec's Reverse slice class expansion section additionally carries a standing scope paragraph so the green mark can never again read as an unqualified outcome guarantee.\n\nSTANDING ENFORCEMENT: emits_enum_members and emits_abstract_members on the G2 matrix. Opened at 8 strict-xfail holes, drained to 0; KNOWN_HOLES is empty and every parametrized cell is a hard lock. Each drain was FORCED to promote its own cell -- xfail(strict=True) means a fixed analyzer XPASSes and fails the run until the entry is removed in the same commit.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM'S FILING GOT WRONG, since the corrections are the reusable part:\n- \"module untestable, 0 nodes\" was a self-analysis artifact; module containment works (ruby 100%, 20+ analyzers emit kind=\"module\"), and the zero came from INV-hojus collapsing Python module-kind into file-kind two weeks before the substrate was taken.\n- The kind axis was the wrong axis. Scala trait worked while rust trait did not; csharp interface worked while typescript interface did not. A per-kind scoping could not express the defect and so could not have closed it.\n- Two live holes sat outside the filed scope entirely: typescript interface and swift protocol. The item read interface as 42.9% effective and treated it as working; that was corpus dilution, not a partial mechanism.\n- Neither the slicer nor the containment linker was at fault as filed. The linker had handled trait/enum/struct containers since 2026-02-08 and roots every container whose members exist. The defect was a missing NODE, not a missing edge.\n\nTWO FINDINGS WORTH MORE THAN THIS ITEM, both now recorded in the gate's own comment:\n1. A container that already emits SOME member kind reads as healthy while another kind is wholly invisible. Java and swift enums emitted their METHODS the entire time; only the cases/constants were missing. This is why the parity fixtures declare enums with members only.\n2. The columns measure ONE ANALYZER IN ISOLATION via span nesting, so a green cell is not evidence that reverse-slice expansion works end to end. \"protocol\" was absent from linkers.containment.CONTAINER_KINDS and from the slicer's set, making Swift/Obj-C protocol containment structurally impossible regardless of emission -- swift read green on the column while the live slice was unmoved at 2 nodes. Fixed in both sets (2 -> 4). Found only by re-running the end-to-end repro instead of certifying from the passing unit test.\n\nAlso corrected along the way: three comments naming \"service\" as a container kind (containment's module docstring, its CONTAINER_KINDS comment, and slice.py citing it as the justification for the slicer's narrower set). None was true -- the ADR-0027 Cluster-D fold made interface + meta[\"framework_role\"]=\"service\" canonical and proto.py emits exactly that.\n\nNOT CLOSED BY THIS ITEM, filed as WI-dorop: scala, cpp and kotlin still emit enum containers with zero members. Re-verified on dev 5eb051e8c7, all three at 0 contains-out in the same run where every drained language reports non-zero. They sit outside the matrix because it is scoped to core-availability analyzers needing no grammar wheel. Kotlin is the subtle one: `enum class Color` is emitted as kind=\"class\", so it will not surface under an enum-kind query at all."  # 2283
